// WHAT ACTUALLY DEPLOYS IS NOT THIS FILE. Read this before "fixing" anything below.
//
// `npm run build` makes @astrojs/cloudflare write `dist/server/wrangler.json` and a redirect at
// `.wrangler/deploy/config.json` pointing at it. Wrangler then reports, verbatim:
//
//   Using redirected Wrangler configuration.
//    - Configuration being used:      dist\server\wrangler.json
//    - Original user's configuration: wrangler.jsonc
//
// So `main` and `assets` here are superseded on every deploy. The generated file sets
// `assets.directory` to `../client` — i.e. dist/client only.
//
// WHY `"directory": "./dist"` BELOW IS NOT A LEAK, measured 2026-09-12 after this was raised as a
// follow-up twice. The scope that would be dangerous is exactly this one: `dist/server/` holds
// `.dev.vars` with SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_KEY in plaintext, so publishing `./dist` as static
// assets would serve them. It cannot happen, for two independent reasons:
//
//   1. The adapter's generated config wins on every build, and `scripts/check-client-bundle.mjs`
//      (`assertAssetScope`, in `npm run ci:gate`) refuses to report clean unless that generated
//      scope is exactly `../client`. A publish with the wrong scope never gets a green gate.
//   2. If the redirect file is removed so that wrangler DOES read this file, it fails before it
//      ever reaches `assets`: `main` is a bare package specifier, and wrangler errors with
//      "The entry-point file at @astrojs/cloudflare/entrypoints/server was not found."
//
// Verified against production the same day: `/_astro/fonts/*.woff2` returns 200, while
// `/server/index.js`, `/server/manifest.mjs`, `/_worker.js` and `/client/...` all return 404 —
// assets are scoped to dist/client, the server bundle is not reachable.
//
// Keep the values as they are. Changing `directory` to `./dist/client` would look tidier and
// would be pure noise: nothing reads it, and the day something does, it fails on `main` first.
{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "pupilownik",
  "main": "@astrojs/cloudflare/entrypoints/server",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-05-08",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  "assets": {
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "directory": "./dist",
    "not_found_handling": "404-page",
  },
  // Workers observability. This is the project's only log sink — there is no logging library, so
  // `console.error` in an endpoint or in `.astro` frontmatter is what reaches it.
  //
  // KNOW WHAT ELSE IS IN HERE, recorded 2026-09-13 during the `invite-page-silent-failures` review.
  // Invocation logs are on (no `logs.invocation_logs: false`) and unsampled, so every request's URL
  // is recorded — and for the caretaker page the URL *is* the credential: `/invite/<token>`. The
  // call-site discipline of never interpolating `token` into a log MESSAGE is still right and still
  // worth keeping, but it does not keep the token out of this sink; only turning invocation logs off
  // would, at the cost of the request-level signal that makes an outage diagnosable at all. Recorded
  // as a knowingly-taken trade-off rather than fixed.
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true,
  },
}
